+1.1 **The zero-face cyclic-order test, with both outside placements.** Name the inside arc $a$ and the outside arc $b$, and write their directed endpoints as $a_-,a_+,b_-,b_+$. Give their common circle the positive boundary orientation of its bounded disk. The endpoints alternate, so the two possible cyclic words starting at $a_-$ are $W_+=(a_-,b_-,a_+,b_+)$ and $W_-=(a_-,b_+,a_+,b_-)$. Put $\chi(W_+)=1$ and $\chi(W_-)=-1$. The precise calibration must include both possible placements of the unbounded face on the outside-arc side: they need not be related by a planar isotopy fixing all endpoint labels. Straighten the circle locally at its four endpoints, with inside $a$ directed from South to North, and put the endpoints of $b$ at West and East. Read the outside arrow at an endpoint germ, as in the stipulated standard picture, rather than at a midpoint of its exterior route. There are four cases. If $b$ runs from West to East above the circle, it enters its head at East leftwards, the cyclic word is $W_-$, and $\kappa=1$. If it runs from West to East below the circle, the head germ is still leftwards, its word is still $W_-$, and $\kappa=1$. If $b$ runs from East to West above the circle, it enters West rightwards, its word is $W_+$, and $\kappa=-1$. If it runs from East to West below the circle, its head germ remains rightwards, its word remains $W_+$, and $\kappa=-1$. The same direction is obtained at the tail germ: outgoing at West is leftwards and outgoing at East rightwards. Thus all four placements give the single formula $\kappa=-\chi$. This table also treats the two possible faces on each side of the circle cut by its surgery arc. Straightening an endpoint germ uses only its transverse attachment on the indicated side and does not change which directed endpoint it is; no isotopy exchanging the two outside-face placements is assumed. [F2, given, construct]
 
 2.1 **Changing the omitted point.** Consider the same zero-face circle on the oriented sphere. Its two complementary sides contain its two surgery arcs. If the two omitted points are on the same side, both planar boundary orientations agree and both presentations call the same arc inside. Their cyclic word and $\kappa$ agree by step 1.1. If the omitted points are on opposite sides, the inside/outside names are exchanged and the positive bounded-side boundary orientation is reversed: the boundary orientations of the two sides of an oriented surface are opposite. Both changes are necessary. For example, an old word $W_+=(a_-,b_-,a_+,b_+)$, read in the reverse orientation starting at $b_-$, is $(b_-,a_-,b_+,a_+)$, which is the new word $W_+$ when $b$ is the new inside arc and $a$ the new outside arc. An old word $W_-=(a_-,b_+,a_+,b_-)$ becomes $(b_-,a_+,b_+,a_-)$, which is the new word $W_-$. Hence $\chi$, and therefore $\kappa$, is unchanged in this case too. This is the full two-case calculation; exchanging only the names without reversing the boundary orientation would give an incorrect sign. The same argument applies to an arbitrary orientation-preserving spherical change of coordinates by transporting the circle and its two sides first. [step 1.1, given, construct]
 
